# {{name}}

An Ananke workflow project scaffolded with `nnke new workflow`.

## Files

| File | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `{{name}}.csproj` | .NET project file — references Ananke packages |
| `Program.cs` | Entry point — loads the manifest, binds code jobs, and runs the workflow |
| `{{name}}.ananke.yml` | **Declarative manifest** — defines the workflow topology and agent configuration (see below) |
| `WorkflowState.cs` | Typed state record passed between jobs |
| `secrets.json` | API keys — never commit this file |

## The `.ananke.yml` manifest

The manifest is a YAML file that describes your workflow in two sections:

### `models`

Named aliases for the LLM models your agents will use:

```yaml
models:
  planner:
    provider: {{provider}}   # openai | anthropic | google
    model: {{model}}         # model name for the chosen provider
```

The `provider` and `model` fields are resolved at load time to `IAgentModel` instances.
API keys come from `secrets.json`, not this file.

### `jobs`

Declares every step in the workflow. Each job is either:

- **`type: agent`** — automatically constructed from the manifest config. Requires a `model` alias and a `system_prompt`.
- **`type: code`** — a custom C# delegate. Must be bound in `Program.cs` via `Bind("job-name", ...)` before the workflow runs.

### `connections`

Describes the execution graph using a simple DSL:

| Syntax | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `a -> b` | Run `b` after `a` |
| `a -> fork(b, c)` | Fan out — run `b` and `c` in parallel after `a` |
| `join(b, c) -> d` | Fan in — run `d` only after both `b` and `c` complete |
| `x -> End` | Terminal edge — workflow finishes when `x` completes |

## Running the workflow

```bash
# Add your API key to secrets.json first
dotnet run
```

To switch provider, pass `--provider` when re-scaffolding, or update `secrets.json` and the `models` section of the manifest manually.
